swer/id #uuid "665f03b3-8fe1-5194-816a-e67e96a71c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} :answer-field/id #uuid "56464aea-61e2-5f04-a701-b6c9323c4b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118808}] :question/id #uuid "daa9792e-2290-5f44-bdfd-5cde9ef8123b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42996" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "802710b7-06ce-5984-836b-24d23e908795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118815} {:answer/id #uuid "88d08bdb-91b6-5612-8866-6c32d2154455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118816} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd3681-8559-584e-8dc4-8e32d28a8f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118817} {:answer/id #uuid "24ae822d-ae24-5f9e-bd9e-7163297a8bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118818} {:answer/id #uuid "4e032d56-fe86-578f-8a9